  "type": "numerical",
  "level": 2,
  "question": {
    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>116</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> of a substance upon dissociation reaction, yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>7</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> of hydrogen, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> of oxygen and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>48</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi></math> of carbon. Given that the atomic masses of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>12</mn></math>, respectively. The data agrees with how many formulae of the following?</p><p>A.&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mi>COOH</mi></math></p><p>B.&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>HCHO</mi></math></p><p>C.&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>OOCH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math></p><p>D.&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mi>CHO</mi></math></p>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "2",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mi>Organic</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Compound</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>containg</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></mrow></mfenced><mover><mo>&#10230;</mo><mrow><mi>dissociati</mi><mo>on</mo></mrow></mover><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>gram</mi></mrow></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mrow><mn>60</mn><mi>gram</mi></mrow></munder><mo>+</mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mrow><mn>48</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mi>gram</mi></mrow></munder></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>%</mo></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></math> in&nbsp;the given organic compound&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mn>116</mn></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>465</mn><mo>%</mo></math></p><p>(A)&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>%</mo></math>&nbsp;of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>60</mn></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>%</mo></math></p><p>(B)&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>%</mo></math>&nbsp;of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>30</mn></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn></math>%</p><p>(c)&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>%</mo></math>&nbsp;of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>6</mn><mn>62</mn></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>677</mn></math>%</p><p>(D)&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>%</mo></math>&nbsp;of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>44</mn></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>09</mn></math>%</p><p>The percentage of Hydrogen in the given organic compound is equal to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>465</mn><mo>%</mo></math>&nbsp;which is approximately equal to the percentage of hydrogen in&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>CH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mi>COOH</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>and</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>HCHO</mi></math>.</p>",
